<Group labelreference="L130" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">#Some comments on the stability anaylisis for this case: This is a stencil that's centered both in space and time. Because of this, in the equations for the error, or more specifically, in the equation for xi, we have terms with xi^i, xi^(i+1) and xi^(i-1). This translates into a quadratic equation for xi, instead of a linear equation like for the FTCS, BTCS and CN cases analyzed in class. We therefore have 2 possible solutions for xi that satisfy the equation. For one of those solutions we find xi=1, but for the other solution we find that the method will only be stable for z=0. Recalling the definition of z=-4sd/h^2, the stability of the method requires that either the time step or the space steps are taken to be zero, but since we approximate derivatives via finite differences, this is never the case. 

#We conclude that the method is unconditionally unstable.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
